<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eurico Gaspar Dutra</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1974">1974</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eurico Gaspar Dutra was a Brazilian general who served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas">Getúlio Vargas</a>'s war minister from 1936 to 1945, supporting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>, and then joined the officers who removed Vargas in 1945. Elected president that year, he governed from 1946 to 1951 under the new democratic constitution of 1946. His government aligned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> and banned the Communist Party in 1947.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eurico-gaspar-dutra.jpg" alt="Eurico Gaspar Dutra" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Governo do Brasil · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GASPARDUTRA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dutra was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuiaba">Cuiabá</a>, Mato Grosso, on 18 May 1883. At the age of 19 he falsified his birth year to 1885 so that his physical condition would match his age and ease his entry into the army. He studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande-do-sul">Rio Grande do Sul</a> and at the Military Academy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, from which he was expelled in 1904 for taking part in an uprising linked to the Vaccine Revolt, but he was pardoned, returned and finished the course in 1906. He graduated first in his class at the School of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a> in 1922.</p><p>His early career was spent largely in putting down revolts. He fought the uprising known as the 18 Fort in 1922, took part in the repression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> revolution of 1924, fought in the Revolution of 1930 and then, as a colonel, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutionalist-revolution">Constitutionalist Revolution</a> in São Paulo in 1932. He stood out in the reaction to the communist rising of 1935, known as the Intentona Comunista, and became Minister of War in 1936, holding the post until 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>During the Second World War Dutra was among the Brazilian military leaders who opposed alignment with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> and a deeper involvement in the conflict. Once <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> had sent forces to fight beside the Allies, and with civil society pressing for democratisation, he accepted the end of the regime begun in 1930 and took part in the deposition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas">Getúlio Vargas</a> in October 1945. Vargas then announced his support for Dutra, the army's candidate, against the air force candidate Eduardo Gomes in the elections that followed.</p><p>The fifth constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> was enacted on 18 September 1946, returning the country to democratic rule. His government closed the casinos and banned gambling, and created the Social Service of Industry and the Social Service of Commerce. In 1947 it broke off diplomatic relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and the Ministry of Labour intervened in many unions. The Salte Plan, proposed that year, concentrated on health, food, transport and energy. Work began on the Paulo Afonso hydroelectric plant and on the highway linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a>, and in 1948 his government set up the Superior School of War with American support.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After leaving the presidency Dutra stayed active in politics and ran again in the indirect presidential election of 1964, when he received only 0.55 per cent of the vote against General Humberto de Alencar Castelo Branco. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> on 11 June 1974, aged 91, and was buried in the São João Batista Cemetery. He is remembered as the first president of the Fourth Brazilian Republic, which followed the Vargas regime.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eurico Gaspar Dutra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the fall of the Estado Novo (Brazil) in 1945, General <b>Eurico Gaspar Dutra</b> was elected president, and a new democratic constitution was adopted in 1946.</blockquote><a href="/state/fourth-brazilian-republic">Fourth Brazilian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuiaba">Cuiabá</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-15.6,-56.1&amp;year=1942" aria-label="Cuiabá on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3019 2743 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Eurico Gaspar Dutra"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3469 3013h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3469 3013h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-15.6,-56.1&amp;year=1942">What was here in 1942? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
